/surname><given-names>Soo Yong</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Zaehres</surname><given-names>Holm</given-names></name><contrib-id authenticated="true" contrib-id-type="orcid">https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8062-8428</contrib-id></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Araúzo-Bravo</surname><given-names>Marcos J</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Kim</surname><given-names>Jae-Ick</given-names></name><contrib-id authenticated="true" contrib-id-type="orcid">https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9705-0394</contrib-id></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Ha</surname><given-names>Yoon</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Schöler</surname><given-names>Hans R</given-names></name><contrib-id authenticated="true" contrib-id-type="orcid">https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7422-8847</contrib-id></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><name><surname>Kim</surname><given-names>Jeong Beom</given-names></name><contrib-id authenticated="true" contrib-id-type="orcid">https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6230-8826</contrib-id><email>jbkim@unist.ac.kr</email></contrib></contrib-group><pub-date publication-format="electronic" date-type="publication"><day>26</day><month>10</month><year>2026</year></pub-date><volume>15</volume><elocation-id>e113550</elocation-id><permissions><copyright-statement>© 2026, Lee et al</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2026</copyright-year><copyright-holder>Lee et al</copyright-holder><ali:free_to_read/><license xlink:href="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"><ali:license_ref>http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This article is distributed under the terms of the <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">Creative Commons Attribution License</ext-link>, which permits unrestricted use and redistribution provided that the original author and source are credited.</license-p></license></permissions><related-article related-article-type="corrected-article" ext-link-type="doi" xlink:href="10.7554/eLife.52069" id="ra1"/><kwd-group kwd-group-type="research-organism"><title>Research organism</title><kwd>Human</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front><body><boxed-text><p> Lee H, Lee HY, Lee BE, Gerovska D, Park SY, Zaehres H, Araúzo-Bravo MJ, Kim J-I, Ha Y, Schöler HR, Kim JB. 2020. Sequentially induced motor neurons from human fibroblasts facilitate locomotor recovery in a rodent spinal cord injury model. <italic>eLife</italic> <bold>9</bold>:e52069. doi: <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.52069">10.7554/eLife.52069</ext-link>.</p><p>Published 23 June 2020 </p></boxed-text><p>In the published article, there were errors in the metadata of the human fibroblast lines, as well as in the statistical values reported in Supplementary file 3.</p><p>To distinguish the two human fibroblast lines used in the study, we designated them as HF1 and HF2, using “HF” as an abbreviation for “human fibroblast”, rather than referring to the cells by their original cell line names.</p><p>Regarding HF1, the original cell line name is F134. We initially understood F134 to be an adult fibroblast line. However, we confirmed that F134 is a human fetal liver fibroblast line upon subsequent review of the relevant source publications.</p><p>Regarding HF2, data for this cell line were added during revision, but its source and catalogue number were inadvertently omitted. We acknowledge this omission and the confusion it may have caused. HF2 corresponds to a human adult dermal fibroblast line AG13004 (Coriell Institute for Medical Research).</p><p>In addition, the SD values in Supplementary file 3 were incorrectly entered during table preparation and have been corrected based on the original data.</p><p><bold>Corrected text in Results:</bold></p><p>To generate induced motor neurons (iMNs) from human fibroblasts,</p><p><bold>Originally published text in Results:</bold></p><p>To generate induced motor neurons (iMNs) from human adult fibroblasts (HF1),</p><p><bold>Corrected text in Materials and methods (Cell culture):</bold></p><p>Human fibroblast lines used in this study were HF1, a fetal liver fibroblast line (F134; Sgodda et al., 2013; Moslem et al., 2017), and HF2, an adult dermal fibroblast line (AG13004; Coriell Institute for Medical Research).</p><p><bold>Originally published text in Materials and methods (Cell culture):</bold></p><p>Human adult fibroblast lines, HF1 and HF2 were obtained from surgical resectates (Singhal et al., 2016), which have been obtained with the informed consent (Haridass et al., 2009).</p><p><bold>Corrected text in Key resources table:</bold></p><table-wrap id="inlinetable1" position="anchor"><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" valign="bottom">Reagent type (species) or resource</th><th align="left" valign="bottom">Designation</th><th align="left" valign="bottom">Source or reference</th><th align="left" valign="bottom">Identifiers</th><th align="left" valign="bottom">Additional information</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" valign="bottom">Cell line (<italic>Homo sapiens</italic>)</td><td align="left" valign="bottom">HF1</td><td align="left" valign="bottom">DOI: <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://doi.org/10.2174/1566524011313050015">http://doi.org/10.2174/1566524011313050015</ext-link>. Sgodda et al., 2013</td><td align="left" valign="bottom"/><td align="left" valign="bottom">Parental cells</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="bottom">Cell line (<italic>Homo sapiens</italic>)</td><td align="left" valign="bottom">HF2</td><td align="left" valign="bottom">Coriell Institute for Medical Research</td><td align="left" valign="bottom"> Cat.# AG13004</td><td align="left" valign="bottom">Parental cells</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><p><bold>Originally published text in Key resourc